WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.160
Hello.

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:02.620
Google Cloud Load
Balancing enables

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:05.036
you to scale your applications
from zero to full-throttle,

00:00:05.036 --> 00:00:06.789
with no pre-warming needed.

00:00:06.789 --> 00:00:09.080
You can distribute your
load-balanced compute resources

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.850
in a single or multiple
regions close to your users

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.444
and meet your high
availability requirements.

00:00:14.444 --> 00:00:16.110
Cloud Load Balancing
puts your resources

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.680
behind a single
anycast IP and allows

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.400
you to scale your
resources up and down

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.690
with intelligent Autoscaling.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:27.990
Cloud Load Balancing can support
HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, and UDP.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.000
We are excited to introduce
a new Load Balancing

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.541
UI that enables you to configure
any of these flavors of load

00:00:32.541 --> 00:00:35.700
balancing intuitively and easily
through a single interface.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.750
With the new UI, you
simply pick the protocol,

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.760
configure the related
parameters, and you're done.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.050
In order to create a
new HTTP load balancer,

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.800
click Create Load Balancer.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.490
Now click Start Configuration
in the HTTP/HTTPS Load Balancing

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:49.990
section.

00:00:54.116 --> 00:00:55.740
Fill in the name of
your load balancer.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:59.660
You can use lowercase
letters, numbers, and hyphens.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.770
Click Backend
Configuration to configure

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:05.030
where your incoming
traffic will be redirected.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.690
From the drop-down
menu, you can select

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.380
an existing backend
service or you can

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.230
create a new backend service.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:14.070
Now enter the name for
your backend service.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.250
From the Backend section,
select an instance group.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.740
Finally, select a health
check from the drop-down

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.720
at the bottom.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.476
Your backends are
now configured.

00:01:28.476 --> 00:01:29.850
The next step is
to determine how

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.310
your traffic will be directed.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:34.570
Click Host and Path Rules
from the left panel.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.200
The first row determines where
any traffic not explicitly

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.050
with a host and path
rule will be sent.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.980
Click Add Host and Path
Rule to add a second row.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.290
Now fill it in.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.410
The third step is
configuring your frontend.

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:51.640
Click Frontend Configuration
to configure the protocol,

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.204
IP address, port, and
certificate, if necessary.

00:01:55.204 --> 00:01:57.370
Once that is done, you can
click Review and Finalize

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.780
to review your configuration.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.130
Finally, click the Create button
to create your load balancer.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:05.540
When the new load
balancer is created,

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.870
you can click its row to
expand it and see its details.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.570
If you want to modify
its configuration,

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:11.987
click the Edit button.

00:02:11.987 --> 00:02:14.070
You can go through the
same steps you went through

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.160
during load balancer creation.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.500
Backend configuration,
host and path rules,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.540
and frontend configuration.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.230
Finally, click the
Update button if you

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.780
want to apply your
changes or click

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.930
Cancel to discard your changes.

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:28.120
In order to create a
new TCP load balancer,

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.570
click Create Load Balancer.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.150
Now click the Start
Configuration button in the TCP

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.160
Load Balancing section.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.400
Fill in the name of
your load balancer.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.740
You can use lowercase
letters, numbers, and hyphens.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.675
Now, click Backend
Configuration to configure

00:02:44.675 --> 00:02:48.200
where your incoming
traffic will be redirected.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.210
Select the region where your
instance group is located.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.250
Select an instance
group you want to use.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:58.460
Select a health check
from the drop-down menu.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.040
Click Frontend
Configuration to configure

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.670
the IP address and port.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:08.820
Click Add Frontend IP and
Port and enter a port number.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.390
Now you can click
Review and Finalize

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:11.598
to review your configuration.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.880
Finally, click the Create
button to create your TCP load

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:17.380
balancer.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:21.890
When the new load
balancer is created,

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.930
you can click its row to
expand it and see its details.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:29.000
You can use the Edit button
to modify the configuration.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.550
You can similarly configure
UDP load balancer, as well.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:39.400
To view all your load
balancers, go to Networking

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.669
and click on Load Balancing.

00:03:42.669 --> 00:03:44.460
Once you've configured
your load balancers,

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.950
you can view them and also
view their details, as well.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.790
We invite you to give
the new Load Balancing

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.270
UI a spin at cloud.google.com.

